1
00:00:00,132 --> 00:00:04,629
What if your customers could close their
own support tickets with a single click?

2
00:00:04,629 --> 00:00:06,738
No reply, no login, no back and forth.

3
00:00:06,818 --> 00:00:11,484
That's Klick-Zen. In the next couple of
minutes, we'll build a Close Ticket button

4
00:00:11,484 --> 00:00:13,590
and put it into a real Zendesk email.

5
00:00:13,670 --> 00:00:17,778
Klick-Zen lives right inside Zendesk. Open
it from the sidebar

6
00:00:17,858 --> 00:00:21,617
Under Available KlickButtons, click
"Create new button".

7
00:00:21,697 --> 00:00:26,880
First, give it an internal name, so your
team can find it later. We'll call this

8
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:27,981
one Ticket Close.

9
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:31,740
Now for the words your customer will see.
Just type right on the button.

10
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:36,505
Close Ticket. Short, clear, and it says
exactly what will happen.

11
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:40,954
It already works, but let's make it look
the part. A friendly green background,

12
00:00:41,034 --> 00:00:44,991
white text, bold lettering, and softer
corners.

13
00:00:45,071 --> 00:00:50,008
Klick-Zen hosts the button image for you,
so it looks exactly like this in every

14
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:50,810
email client.

15
00:00:50,890 --> 00:00:56,272
Next, tell the button what to do. Click
Add button action, and choose Set Ticket

16
00:00:56,272 --> 00:00:56,743
Status.

17
00:00:56,823 --> 00:01:00,642
Set the new status to Solved. That's the
whole workflow.

18
00:01:00,722 --> 00:01:05,360
Does your Zendesk require an assignee
before a ticket can be solved? Add one

19
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:09,449
here, and turn on If Blank, so existing
assignments stay untouched.

20
00:01:09,529 --> 00:01:14,716
Click Create. Klick-Zen saves your button
and generates a ready-to-paste snippet.

21
00:01:14,796 --> 00:01:17,578
Copy it with one click.

22
00:01:17,658 --> 00:01:22,299
Now put it where your customers will see
it. In Zendesk, open a trigger that emails

23
00:01:22,299 --> 00:01:23,082
the requester.

24
00:01:23,162 --> 00:01:28,026
Write your message, then paste the snippet
right below it.

25
00:01:28,106 --> 00:01:31,223
Save the trigger, and you're live.

26
00:01:31,303 --> 00:01:36,013
Here's what your customer receives. A
friendly message, and one obvious button.

27
00:01:36,093 --> 00:01:40,270
One click, and they land on a confirmation
page. Nothing else to do.

28
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:45,963
And back in Zendesk, the ticket is already
solved. No reply to read, and no agent

29
00:01:45,963 --> 00:01:46,725
time spent.

30
00:01:46,805 --> 00:01:50,055
That's your first Klick-Zen button, built
in minutes.

31
00:01:50,135 --> 00:01:55,292
From here, you can offer a Still need help
choice, make buttons single use, or follow

32
00:01:55,292 --> 00:01:57,719
up with a one-click satisfaction survey.

33
00:01:57,799 --> 00:02:02,654
Find Klick-Zen on the Zendesk Marketplace,
and let your customers close the loop with

34
00:02:02,654 --> 00:02:03,511
a single click.
